\begin{proposition}[Bellman equations, \LeanResult{prop:bellman}]\label{prop:bellman}
For a finite \ac{mdp} with $\gamma\in[0,1)$, define
\[
Q_V(s,a)\coloneqq R(s,a)+\gamma\sum_{s'}\P(s'\mid s,a)V(s').
\]
Then $V^{\pi}$ and $V^{\star}$ uniquely solve, respectively,
\begin{align}
V(s)&=\sum_{a\in A(s)}\pi(a\mid s)Q_V(s,a), &&s\in S, \label{eq:bellman_evaluation}\\
V(s)&=\max_{a\in A(s)}Q_V(s,a), &&s\in S. \label{eq:bellman_optimality}
\end{align}
The first is a linear system; the second is the \emph{Bellman optimality equation}. Any pure policy $\polstar$ selecting a maximizer of $Q_{V^{\star}}$ in each state is optimal: $V^{\polstar}=V^{\star}$.
\end{proposition}

changes.before:
\begin{proof}
Both claims are standard \parencite{Puterman1994mdp,SuttonBarto2018rl}. The right-hand sides of \eqref{eq:bellman_evaluation} and \eqref{eq:bellman_optimality} define $\gamma$-contractions on $\bigl(\RR^{S}, \norm{\cdot}_{\infty}\bigr)$, so each has a unique fixed point by the Banach fixed-point theorem; conditioning \eqref{eq:mdp_value} on the first transition identifies these fixed points as $V^{\pi}$ and $V^{\star}$, and any policy greedy for $V^{\star}$ is optimal. The acyclic games below make even the contraction argument unnecessary (Remark~\ref{rem:structural_discount}).
\end{proof}

