\begin{proof}
Induct backward along a topological order of the finite acyclic graph. At terminal states, \eqref{eq:shapley_bellman} fixes $V^{\star}(s)=R(s)$ and \eqref{eq:saddle_point} holds. For $s\in S_+$, assume uniqueness and the saddle inequalities at all successors. Each action's bracket in \eqref{eq:shapley_bellman} is finite; maximizing over the finite action set uniquely defines $V^{\star}(s)$ and supplies a pure choice $\polstar_+(s)$. For any opponent policy $\pi_-$, condition on the first transition and apply the successors' left saddle inequality with $\gamma(s)\ge0$:
\begin{align*}
	V^{(\polstar_{+}, \pi_{-})}(s)
	&= R\bigl(s, \polstar_{+}(s)\bigr) + \gamma(s) \sum_{s'} \P\bigl(s' \mid s, \polstar_{+}(s)\bigr)\, V^{(\polstar_{+}, \pi_{-})}(s') \\
	&\ge R\bigl(s, \polstar_{+}(s)\bigr) + \gamma(s) \sum_{s'} \P\bigl(s' \mid s, \polstar_{+}(s)\bigr)\, V^{\star}(s')
	\;=\; V^{\star}(s),
\end{align*}
The last equality uses the maximizing choice $\polstar_+(s)$. Against $\polstar_-$, any $\pi_+$ averages the bracket to at most its maximum; conditioning and the successors' right saddle inequality give $V^{(\pi_+,\polstar_-)}(s)\le V^{\star}(s)$. For $s\in S_-$, exchange $\max$ with $\min$ and reverse the inequalities. Taking $\pi=\polstar$ in both inequalities gives $V^{\polstar}=V^{\star}$. The argument holds in every rooted subgame, proving subgame perfection.
\end{proof}

changes.after:
\begin{proof}
Backward induction on the finite acyclic graph starts from $V^{\star}(s)=R(s)$ at terminal states. At each remaining state, the known successor values determine $Q_{V^{\star}}$; its finite nonempty action set has an attained maximum or minimum, uniquely determining the value and a pure optimal choice.

For the saddle inequalities, induct in the same order. At $s\in S_+$, playing the maximizing action against any $\pi_-$ gives at least $V^{\star}(s)$ by the successors' left inequality and $\gamma(s)\ge0$. Conversely, any $\pi_+$ against $\polstar_-$ gives at most the average of $Q_{V^{\star}}(s,\cdot)$, hence at most its maximum $V^{\star}(s)$, by the successors' right inequality. At $s\in S_-$ the argument exchanges maximum and minimum. With both players optimal, the inequalities give $V^{\polstar}=V^{\star}$. Since this holds at every state, the equilibrium is subgame perfect.
\end{proof}

